nodecode-dpapi-secrets is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit 5bad3fb2baf04ce6befecfefce6d14f4c9c98934
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

The original is one Python plugin folder for Hermes Agent (`dpapi-secrets/`). It
registered a secret source named `dpapi`: at startup `fetch()` read one file,
`HERMES-DPAPI-1` and a DPAPI blob, decrypted it with `CryptProtectData`'s
counterpart through ctypes, parsed the JSON `{name: value}` inside and answered
the variables the `secrets.dpapi.map` config named -- warnings for the ones the
store could not answer, an error and nothing injected when none could. Its own
part was the file format and its header, the ctypes prototypes, the user-scope
flag, the config parser and its warnings, the error kinds (NOT_CONFIGURED,
BINARY_MISSING, AUTH_FAILED, EMPTY_VALUE) and the words each one gets, and a
provisioning CLI (`set`, `unset`, `list`, `path`) that wrote the store
atomically and never put a value on a command line or in the clear.

This rewrite keeps the store, the format and the protections as a cell:
`dpapi_list` is the same store read for names, `dpapi_show` one entry's value,
`dpapi_env` the mapping the original injected, `dpapi_status` the protection
and the count, `dpapi_set` and `dpapi_unset` the CLI's two writes, and
`/dpapi-secrets` them in one command. The `HERMES-DPAPI-1` header and its
`{name: value}` payload are the original's, so a store the plugin wrote is read
here unchanged; the DPAPI calls are SBCL alien calls with every argument typed,
as the original typed every ctypes argument; the user-scope flag, the UI-forbidden
flag, the fail-closed reads, the atomic write and the config warnings are its
rules reimplemented in Common Lisp. `(secret NAME)` reads one entry for a form
instead of an environment variable, and `(resolve)` answers the whole mapping.

Left out, and why: Hermes' secret-source seam itself, which Nodecode does not
have -- so a value is read when a tool asks for it rather than injected at
startup, nothing is written into the process environment, and the original's
precedence rules have nothing to act on. The bundled CLI's hidden-prompt and
stdin forms are the shell's job here: `/dpapi-secrets set` asks through a
dialog. Nothing outside Windows can run DPAPI, so on another platform the cell
writes the same map under a header of its own, a 0600 file, and says so
everywhere it is asked -- the original simply refused to resolve anything
there. The original's `timeout_seconds` has no work to bound and is not
carried. DPAPI and the Data Protection API are Microsoft's; the license above
covers the plugin, not the platform.
